  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 PM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nPartly to mostly cloudy skies  this afternoon with temperatures \ngenerally in the mid to upper 80s, and only isolated showers noted on\nradar, with most locations remaining dry through early evening.\n\nCAMs continue to show a line  of showers and thunderstorms \ndeveloping late tonight into early Wednesday morning along the nose \nof a developing low-level jet, generally along and south of a line \nfrom Belleville to Manhattan, Topeka and Ottawa. Severe storms are \nnot favored with a decoupled boundary layer and lack of shear, but \noccasional gusty winds are possible due to precip loading and \nstronger downdrafts potentially pushing through the inversion. \nAdditionally, heavy rain is possible with PWATs in excess of 2.25 \ninches and potential training of thunderstorms. While flooding is not\nwidely expected with less focused lift and a more scattered storm \nmode, training of storms could lead to some localized flooding. \n\nBeyond tonight, guidance keeps  highs near seasonal normals in the \nupper 80s to near 90 through the remainder of the work week with \nperiodic low-end showers and thunderstorm chances as weak \ndisturbances ride northwest flow aloft. A stronger cold front is \nfavored to swing through Thursday night into Friday. This is likely \nour best chance at more widespread rain and stronger storms.",
